{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>And Elohim saw all that He had made, and behold, it was very good.</b> Even though He already mentioned 'for it was good' at the creation of each and every day, He nevertheless returned here and said 'and behold, it was good' after the creation of man, to indicate that all the earlier creatures were created only for the sake of man. And even though it was said of them 'for it was good,' it was said on account of the future, but they were not yet in their fulfillment and their goodness until man was created — for whose sake they were all brought into being; then He saw and declared 'and behold, it was very good.' And know that there is a difference between 'for it was good' and 'and behold, it was very good,' for the expression 'for' (כִּי) is one of doubt, as though it were not certain — for everywhere the expression 'for' (כִּי) denotes contingency, because the word 'כִּי' points to time, as in 'כִּי תֵצֵא' ('when you go out'), 'כִּי תָבוֹא' ('when you come'). And 'Elohim saw that it was good' means that He was waiting for the time when their goodness would be revealed, but 'and behold, it was good' denotes certainty. And our Sages of blessed memory said in the Midrash (Genesis Rabbah 8:5) that the Holy One, blessed be He, was debating with the angels whether to create man or not, and had the judgment gone to not create man, the existence of all the earlier creatures would have been for naught. Therefore 'for it was good' was said of them — denoting doubt — but when man was created, then they became certainly good; therefore it says 'all that He had made, and behold, it was good' — the expression 'and behold' denotes the present time in which man is seen, and then it becomes evident retroactively what the goodness and what the beauty of all the earlier creatures is. And He added here 'very' (מְאֹד), which He had not mentioned before, to indicate that through man they became 'very good.' A hint to this matter: מְאֹ״ד — the letters of אָדָם.\"" "And one may explain further, by the parable of a meal of meat and of fish: each is good on its own, but if they are mixed together they spoil. But these creatures — not only was each one good on its own; even in their joining together, all of them were good as well. And this is what it says: ״כָּל אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה וְהִנֵּה טוֹב מְאֹד״ (Genesis 1:31)." "<b>The sixth day.</b> He added the letter ה in 'the sixth' to say that the Holy One, blessed be He, made a condition with them — on condition that Israel accept the five fifths of the Torah, or else all of you are suspended until the sixth day, which is Sivan. For the ה in its full spelling (מִלּוּאוֹ) equals six [i.e., ה = 5, plus the two 'filler' letters א and א = 1+1, but actually מלואו של ה׳ is הא״א, whose letters sum to 6]. And the reason for this is that the upper ones and the lower ones are two opposites and cannot endure except by means of some intermediary that joins and connects these two opposing parts — and this is man, in whom there is a physical part and a spiritual part, a divine part from above. And the endurance of the spiritual part depends on the receiving of the Torah. If so, had Israel not received the Torah there would have been no intermediary at all to connect the two opposing extremes, and necessity would have demanded returning the world to תֹהוּ וָבֹהוּ. And do not answer me from the time before the giving of the Torah, for there were always righteous people in the world engaged in Torah — Noah, Shem, and Eber, the Patriarchs, and their like — and they had with them the great Name השם, which sums to twenty-six. Therefore the world stood for twenty-six generations without Torah among the multitudes; but after twenty-six generations, when the numerical count of the Name was completed, the isolated individuals of the generation no longer had the power to sustain the worlds except through the receiving of the Torah. Therefore one who engages in Torah brings peace in the retinue above and below (Sanhedrin 99a). And this matter will be explained further (Deuteronomy 32:1) with the help of HaShem." "And as for why the world was created in specifically six days, the commentators said that it alludes to the duration of this world's standing, which is six thousand years: two thousand years of תֹהוּ, without Torah — corresponding to this, there are two letters aleph in the verse ״וְהָאָרֶץ הָיְתָה תֹהוּ״. Two thousand years of Torah — corresponding to this, there are two letters aleph in the verse (Exodus 13:9) ״לְמַעַן תִּהְיֶה תּוֹרַת ה׳ בְּפִיךָ״. Two thousand years of the days of the Messiah — corresponding to this, there are two letters aleph in the verse (Genesis 49:10) ״עַד כִּי יָבֹא שִׁילֹה״ — and this is correct. And other commentators examined the creation of each and every day as corresponding to one thousand years, one after another, and there is no need to elaborate on them. And some say that the letter ה was added in 'the sixth' because the world was created with the letter ה, and after completing the work the Holy One, blessed be He, left the tools of His craft on the sixth day and did not want them to be with Him on the Sabbath — so that people would learn from this that every craftsman should leave his craft behind on the sixth [day]." "<b>The sixth day — and the heavens were finished.</b> He mentioned the Name that is formed by the initial letters of these four words, corresponding to what I explained above regarding the twenty-six generations. And according to the plain sense, He sealed the entire work of Creation with the Name יָהּ, which is the Name shared between man and wife, corresponding to the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Bava Batra 74a) that everything the Holy One, blessed be He, created in His world, He created male and female. And it says (Isaiah 26:4) ״כִּי בְּיָ״הּ ה׳ צוּר עוֹלָמִים״ — which means by this that everything He created in this world has in it the likeness of male and female together, for every male is likened to the giver of influence and every female to the receiver of influence, like the female who receives from the man, and all created beings have in them one aspect that gives influence and one aspect that receives — all as one. How so? The Holy One, blessed be He, is the first giver of influence, bestowing utterances of abundance upon the upper world, but He, blessed be He, does not receive from any other. And the upper world in turn gives influence to the middle world; thus the upper world has in it the likeness of male and female together, for it receives as a female from the first cause, blessed be He, and gives influence as a male to the middle world. And likewise the middle world receives influence from the upper world and in turn gives influence to the lower world; if so, the middle world too is called male and female together — giving and receiving. And the lower ones, even though they receive and do not give influence, nevertheless they also have in them a likeness of the male, because all existent beings below need one another — some give and some receive — and among them there are also actual male and female, as with all living creatures, until it is applicable to all of them to say that they receive and give influence as one. Only to HaShem alone can one attribute the designation of 'man' (אִישׁ) exclusively — giving influence and not receiving. This is what our Sages of blessed memory said in several Midrashim (Sotah 42b): ״אֵין אִישׁ אֶלָּא הַקָּדוֹשׁ בָּרוּךְ הוּא, שֶׁנֶּאֱמַר 'ה׳ אִישׁ מִלְחָמָה'״ — which means that one cannot attribute the designation of 'man' (אִישׁ), which denotes the giver of influence who does not receive, to any but HaShem alone, for all of them need Him, blessed be He, and He, blessed be He, does not need any one of them." "And that which we find the term אִישׁ ('man') applied to the creatures is not essentially so, but only by way of borrowing, in the coupling of אִישׁ and אִשָּׁה ('man and woman'); but the essential name אִישׁ can be attributed only to Him, blessed be He. Therefore He sealed all the work of Creation on the sixth day with the name of יָהּ, which is shared between אִישׁ and אִשָּׁה — to indicate that all of them, male and female, HaShem created. Therefore in this world the creatures do not use the full name, but only the name יָהּ; but in the World to Come — the day that is entirely Sabbath — then they will use the full name, for ״לֹא הַמֵּתִים יְהַלְלוּ יָהּ״ (the dead do not praise Yah). Therefore the letter ו״ה is hinted at at the beginning of these words: ״וַיְכֻלּוּ הַשָּׁמַיִם״ — which speaks of the day of Sabbath in the revealed sense and hints in hidden ways to the day that is entirely Sabbath, for the World to Come, to indicate that then the Name will be full. And so David sealed the psalm ״בָּרְכִי נַפְשִׁי״, which speaks entirely of the work of Creation, with the name of יָהּ — and see further in the verse ״כִּי בִּגְלַל הַדָּבָר הַזֶּה״ (Deuteronomy 15:10)."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {5 "The verse citation ״לֹא הַמֵּתִים יְהַלְלוּ יָהּ״ is from Psalms but is given here without address, so left unaddressed per instructions. The citation marked (דברים טו:י) appears twice; the second occurrence after ״כִּי בִּגְלַל הַדָּבָר הַזֶּה״ with the parenthetical (טו:י) is rendered exactly as in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:34:24.908830516Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}